packages in reducing maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality; and improving neonatal outcomes.", "background": 
"While maternal, infant and under‐five child mortality rates in developing countries have declined significantly in the 
past two to three decades, newborn mortality rates have reduced much more slowly. While it is recognised that almost 
half of the newborn deaths can be prevented by scaling up evidence‐based available interventions (such as tetanus toxoid
immunisation to mothers, clean and skilled care at delivery, newborn resuscitation, exclusive breastfeeding, clean 
umbilical cord care, and/or management of infections in newborns), many require facility‐based and outreach services. It
has also been stated that a significant proportion of these mortalities and morbidities could also be potentially 
addressed by developing community‐based packaged interventions which should also be supplemented by developing and 
strengthening linkages with the local health systems. Some of the recent community‐based studies of interventions 
targeting women of reproductive age have shown variable impacts on maternal outcomes and hence it is uncertain if these 
